Subject: [flux] The browser shell: a full-height console column

The console had no home in the window: chrome://flux was reachable by URL and
nothing else. The design has it as a 305px column starting at the top of the
window, with the tab strip beginning to its right. That is not a side panel -
a side panel sits below the toolbar, inside the content area, and no amount of
configuring one gets it above the tab strip.

Chromium's own layout is left completely alone. BrowserViewLayout::CreateLayout
already exists to swap layouts by browser type, so a normal window now gets
FluxBrowserViewLayout: it overrides DoPreLayoutComputations() to narrow the
params before handing them to the tabbed layout, and everything downstream -
tab strip, toolbar, contents, side panel - measures and places itself in what
is left, with no knowledge that a sidebar exists. Popups, app windows and
devtools are untouched; they have no console.

The view is a child of BrowserView rather than of the contents container, which
is what makes it a sibling of the tab strip rather than of the page. It hosts
the same chrome://flux WebUI as the tab, serving sidebar.html, so there is one
data source, one Mojo interface and one stylesheet behind both surfaces.

The two new files live under //chrome/browser/flux but compile into
//chrome/browser/ui: FluxBrowserViewLayout derives from
BrowserViewTabbedLayoutImpl and browser_view.cc constructs FluxSidebarView, so
a target of their own would be a cycle with this one.

